<article class="env-lemma" id="0EYE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EYE">Lemma <span data-tag="0EYE">58.31.3</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a locally Noetherian scheme. Let $D \subset X$ be an effective Cartier divisor such that $D$ is a regular scheme. Let $Y \to X \setminus D$ be a finite étale morphism. If $Y$ is unramified over $X$ in codimension $1$, then there exists a finite étale morphism $Y' \to X$ whose restriction to $X \setminus D$ is $Y$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is a special case of Lemma <a href="/tag/0H2W" data-tag="0H2W">58.31.2</a>. First, $D$ is nowhere dense in $X$ (see discussion in Divisors, Section <a href="/tag/01WQ" data-tag="01WQ">31.14</a>) and hence $X \setminus D$ is dense in $X$. Second, the ring $\mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ is a regular local ring for all $x \in D$ by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00NU" data-tag="00NU">10.106.7</a> and our assumption that $\mathcal{O}_{D, x}$ is regular. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>